; =====================================================================
; rfc8610-originals.abnf
;
; VERBATIM.  RFC 9682's own quotations of the RFC 8610 grammar it
; replaces, copied unchanged from RFC 9682 and NOT authored by this
; project.  Five of the six regions carry the source's own leading
; comment line, "; ABNF from RFC 8610:" or "; extracted from the ABNF in
; RFC 8610:", which is part of the displayed figure.
;
; Locator:  RFC 9682, "Updates to the Concise Data Definition Language
;           (CDDL) Grammar", C. Bormann, November 2024, Standards
;           Track, ISSN 2070-1721.  Updates: 8610.
;           <https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9682.txt>
;
; Source digest (sha256 of the full cached source text):
;   d35602e7f784e509a5984283ea8d998adfee23f086ad0d77bd22f98c6cdea89c
;
; WHY THIS FILE EXISTS.  The production-by-production change table in
; production-changes.derived.txt needs a left-hand column: what RFC 8610
; said before.  That column is sourced HERE, from RFC 9682's own
; reproduction of it, not from this library's rfc-8610 record -- so the
; change table stands on one document's bytes and can be checked against
; one digest.  Where a reader wants RFC 8610's complete Appendix B
; rather than the six fragments RFC 9682 quotes, it is captured verbatim
; at ../../../rfc-8610/distilled/schema/cddl.abnf.
;
; CROSS-CHECK PERFORMED.  Sixteen rule statements appear in the six
; regions below.  FIFTEEN are byte-identical, after dedenting and after
; stripping trailing ABNF comments, to the same rule in the rfc-8610
; record's verbatim Appendix B capture: text, SCHAR, SESC (Figure 1),
; bytes, BCHAR, bsqual (Figure 3), cddl (Figure 7), bytes, BCHAR again
; (Figure 12), and WS, SP, NL, COMMENT, PCHAR, CRLF (Figure 14).
; RFC 9682 quotes its predecessor exactly; it does not paraphrase it.
; Figure 12 and Figure 3 overlap -- both quote `bytes` and the old
; `BCHAR` -- and they agree with each other and with RFC 8610.
;
; THE SIXTEENTH IS NOT A FULL RULE AND MUST NOT BE READ AS ONE.
; Figure 9 is an EXTRACT, and the source says so in its own lead-in
; comment: "; extracted from the ABNF in RFC 8610:".  It prints
;
;      type2 =/ "#" "6" ["." uint] "(" S type S ")"
;
; using ABNF's incremental-alternative operator `=/`, where RFC 8610
; Appendix B defines `type2` with `=` and eleven alternatives.  The
; alternative quoted here IS present verbatim in RFC 8610 Appendix B, on
; its own line `           / "#" "6" ["." uint] "(" S type S ")"` --
; checked -- but the STATEMENT `type2 =/ ...` is RFC 9682's framing of
; it, not a line of RFC 8610.  A reader who concatenated this file's six
; regions would get a `type2` with one alternative.  Use
; ../../../rfc-8610/distilled/schema/cddl.abnf for RFC 8610's whole
; grammar; use cddl-updated.abnf for the grammar in force.
; (Figure 10, the replacement, uses `=/` in the same way, and Figure 11
; resolves both into `type2`'s single `=` definition.)
;
; Figure captions, quoted:
;   Figure 1   "Original ABNF from RFC 8610 for Strings with Permissive
;               ABNF for SESC (Which Did Not Allow Hex Escapes)"
;   Figure 3   "ABNF from RFC 8610 for BCHAR"
;   Figure 7   "ABNF from RFC 8610 for Top-Level Rule cddl"
;   Figure 9   "Original ABNF from RFC 8610 for Tag Syntax"
;   Figure 12  "Original ABNF from RFC 8610 for BCHAR"
;   Figure 14  "ABNF Definition of WS from RFC 8610"
;
; NOTHING IN THIS FILE IS CURRENT.  It is the superseded text.  The
; grammar in force is cddl-updated.abnf.
; =====================================================================

; ---- BEGIN VERBATIM FIGURE-1 (text, SCHAR, SESC as RFC 8610 had them) (source lines 123-126, sha256 7598acd2774c6a440a4653452daf80df2d0b7f9015c9046f66943a8a93703847) ----
   ; ABNF from RFC 8610:
   text = %x22 *SCHAR %x22
   SCHAR = %x20-21 / %x23-5B / %x5D-7E / %x80-10FFFD / SESC
   SESC = "\" (%x20-7E / %x80-10FFFD)
; ---- END VERBATIM FIGURE-1 (text, SCHAR, SESC as RFC 8610 had them) ----

; ---- BEGIN VERBATIM FIGURE-3 (bytes, BCHAR, bsqual as RFC 8610 had them) (source lines 171-174, sha256 467a92d3258d9a531f425b38b86bbc67ef9af0f8e47d0339500ee16e31c7d5aa) ----
   ; ABNF from RFC 8610:
   bytes = [bsqual] %x27 *BCHAR %x27
   BCHAR = %x20-26 / %x28-5B / %x5D-10FFFD / SESC / CRLF
   bsqual = "h" / "b64"
; ---- END VERBATIM FIGURE-3 (bytes, BCHAR, bsqual as RFC 8610 had them) ----

; ---- BEGIN VERBATIM FIGURE-7 (cddl as RFC 8610 had it) (source lines 279-280, sha256 e37a752c6359f10288f033d6d1cb987b71b0261ab99858e157f6e2f6d38c4ef9) ----
   ; ABNF from RFC 8610:
   cddl = S 1*(rule S)
; ---- END VERBATIM FIGURE-7 (cddl as RFC 8610 had it) ----

; ---- BEGIN VERBATIM FIGURE-9 (type2 tag alternative as RFC 8610 had it) (source lines 307-308, sha256 39c599884002eb0f8ded79803d536b942e6523614328281cdae6605359367d61) ----
   ; extracted from the ABNF in RFC 8610:
   type2 =/ "#" "6" ["." uint] "(" S type S ")"
; ---- END VERBATIM FIGURE-9 (type2 tag alternative as RFC 8610 had it) ----

; ---- BEGIN VERBATIM FIGURE-12 (bytes, BCHAR again, in Appendix B) (source lines 604-606, sha256 a1ed57f1275a5ef4020c65dbafc79fb3907e96850d8dc600f2c06c8e48de4f13) ----
   ; ABNF from RFC 8610:
   bytes = [bsqual] %x27 *BCHAR %x27
   BCHAR = %x20-26 / %x28-5B / %x5D-10FFFD / SESC / CRLF
; ---- END VERBATIM FIGURE-12 (bytes, BCHAR again, in Appendix B) ----

; ---- BEGIN VERBATIM FIGURE-14 (the WS block as RFC 8610 had it) (source lines 628-634, sha256 a1c5993769d6d9939b6d189a49317052e4c5f479f42b44b0b255bb73ca2cb98b) ----
   ; ABNF from RFC 8610:
   WS = SP / NL
   SP = %x20
   NL = COMMENT / CRLF
   COMMENT = ";" *PCHAR CRLF
   PCHAR = %x20-7E / %x80-10FFFD
   CRLF = %x0A / %x0D.0A
; ---- END VERBATIM FIGURE-14 (the WS block as RFC 8610 had it) ----
